% ch-04-ex-02-compound-circuits.glp
%
% This file presents the §4.1 compound-circuit Programs from book pp 29–30
% (PDF pp 41–42). All clauses byte-exact from the PDF.
%
% Programs included:
%   §4.1 Clauses with Bodies (book p 29): nand/3 — first clause-with-body
%   §4.1 Guards for Multiple Reader Occurrences (book p 29–30): half_adder/4
%     — uses `ground` guards because A? and B? each appear twice in the body
%   §4.1 Compound Circuits (book p 30): full_adder/5 — composes two
%     half_adders + or; each reader appears only once in the body
%
% Plus the duplicated logic gates (and/3, or/3, not/2, xor/3) from ex-01,
% included inline per FR-010 self-containment so this file is loadable in a
% fresh REPL session without ex-01.
%
% References Formal 4.1 (Produces and Consumes Parameters, book p 29) and
% Formal 4.3 (Which Guards Enable Multiple Reader Occurrences, book p 35–36).
%
% The pedagogical theme of this exercise is the composition of unit-clause
% gates (from ex-01) into clauses-with-bodies that compute compound circuits.
% The `ground` guard on multi-reader clauses (half_adder) is the key
% language feature ex-01 didn't yet exercise.

%% §4.1 Logic Gates (book p 28) — duplicated inline from ex-01 per FR-010.

%% AND truth table.
and(1,1,1).
and(1,0,0).
and(0,1,0).
and(0,0,0).

%% OR truth table.
or(1,1,1).
or(1,0,1).
or(0,1,1).
or(0,0,0).

%% NOT truth table.
not(1,0).
not(0,1).

%% XOR truth table.
xor(1,1,0).
xor(1,0,1).
xor(0,1,1).
xor(0,0,0).

%% §4.1 Clauses with Bodies (book p 29). A NAND gate inverts the output of
%% an AND gate. Per Formal 4.1: third parameter Z? is a reader in the head
%% (writer Z in body produces the value); A and B are writers in the head
%% (paired readers A?, B? in body consume the input values).
nand(A,B,Z?) :- and(A?,B?,W), not(W?,Z).

%% §4.1 Guards for Multiple Reader Occurrences (book p 29–30). The half_adder
%% takes two input bits and produces a sum and carry. The `ground(A?)` and
%% `ground(B?)` guards are necessary because A? and B? each appear twice in
%% the body; the guards ensure both inputs are fully bound before the body
%% executes (per Formal 4.3 — type-test guards permit multiple reader
%% occurrences on a single clause).
half_adder(A,B,Sum?,Carry?) :-
    ground(A?), ground(B?) |
    xor(A?,B?,Sum), and(A?,B?,Carry).

%% §4.1 Compound Circuits (book p 30). The full_adder adds three bits (A, B,
%% Cin) and produces a sum bit and a carry-out. Composes two half_adders +
%% one or-gate. Each reader appears only once in the body, so no `ground`
%% guards are needed (cf. half_adder above).
full_adder(A,B,Cin,Sum?,Cout?) :-
    half_adder(A?,B?,S1,C1),
    half_adder(S1?,Cin?,Sum,C2),
    or(C1?,C2?,Cout).
